// The DF-226f refusal is keyed on the static's TYPE, not on its initializer, so
// a bare `None` meets exactly the same rule a bare payload value does.
//
// This is the row that shows the rule is not a repackaged type mismatch: `None`
// IS assignable to `Int?`, so nothing here is ill-typed in the old sense. The
// declaration is refused because an always-absent immortal value is not a thing
// worth writing — the compile-time answer is `None` forever.
//
// The no-initializer spelling `static SLOT: Int?` lands on the same message,
// and so does an ALIAS to an optional (`type MaybeInt = Int?`), since the
// alias resolves to the static's own top-level type.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: static `SLOT` may not have an optional type (`Int?`)
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: `static X: Once<T>`

static SLOT: Int? = None

func main() {
    print("done")
}
